Mutually exclusive events 🎲

There are 6 only possible outcomes in a rolling a fair, 6-sided die.
What is the probability of rolling a 1 OR a 6?
A single roll cannot produce both 1 and 6 = mutually exclusive event

P(\text{roll 1}) = \frac{1}{6}
P(\text{roll 6}) = \frac{1}{6}


The probability of A OR B is called the “union”, represented by \cup 
P(\text{roll 1} \cup \text{roll 6}) = \frac{1}{6} + \frac{1}{6}  = \frac{2}{6}







Tip


When two events are mutually exclusive (cannot happen together), add their probabilities to calculate the probability of either event occurring.

Non-mutually exclusive events 🎲
What is the probability of Event A or B taking place?


Event A
Roll a 1 or 6

P(\text{A}) = \frac{2}{6}


Event B
Roll a 2, 4 or 6

P(\text{B}) = \frac{3}{6}


Shared outcome
Roll a 6

P(\text{A} \cap \text{B}) = \frac{1}{6}



\begin{aligned}
P(\text{A} \cup \text{B})
  &= P(\text{A}) + P(\text{B}) - P(\text{A} \cap \text{B}) \\
  &= \frac{2}{6} + \frac{3}{6} - \frac{1}{6} \\
  &= \frac{4}{6}
\end{aligned}







Solution


We need to subtract the intersection from the calculation to avoid double counting.

Bringing it together
What is the probability of Event A and B taking place?



\underbrace{P(A \cup B)}_{\text{Union}}












=\underbrace{P(A) + P(B)}_{\text{Marginal probabilities}}












-\underbrace{P(A \cap B)}_{\text{Joint probability}}

















Tip


For mutually exclusive events, the joint probability is 0. That’s why we can simply add the marginal probabilies up!

Learning outcomes
On successful completion of this unit, you should be able to:

classify different data types and apply appropriate visualisation tools for effective data presentation;
apply probability calculations and interpretations in a given business problem;
apply random sampling concepts to interpret statistics calculated from business data and conduct hypothesis testing;
analyse relationships between business data using appropriate tools;
